\b Figure Descriptions for the School Violation System\b0\par
\par
\b Objectives 1, 2, and 3\b0\par
\par
Prepared for Capstone Documentation\par
\par
This section presents the figure descriptions for the major modules of the School Violation System based on the objectives of the study. Each figure explains the purpose of the module and how it functions in supporting the recording, monitoring, retrieval, and reporting of student disciplinary cases.\par
\par
\b Objective 1: Recording, Storing, and Retrieving Student Violation Records\b0\par
\par
\b Figure 25. Log a Violation\b0\par
Figure 25 shows the Log a Violation module of the School Violation System. This module is used to record a new student disciplinary incident in a systematic and organized manner. Through this feature, the administrator selects the student involved, chooses the specific violation committed, and enters important incident details such as the date, time, witness, and description. The system is designed to guide the administrator step by step, making the recording process easier, more accurate, and less prone to error. Once the necessary information is entered, the system automatically checks the student's previous violation history to determine the corresponding offense level and suggested sanction. After submission, the violation record is saved in the centralized database with an initial case status, allowing it to be monitored, reviewed, and updated in the later stages of the disciplinary process.\par
\par
\b Figure 26. Auto Sanction Preview\b0\par
Figure 26 shows the Auto Sanction Preview feature of the School Violation System. This feature is used to help the administrator identify the appropriate disciplinary action before finalizing a student violation record. Through this feature, the system automatically analyzes the selected student and the chosen violation, then checks the student's previous offense history to determine the current offense level and the corresponding sanction. This allows the administrator to verify the expected disciplinary action before the case is officially recorded. By previewing the sanction in advance, the system promotes consistency, fairness, and accuracy in applying disciplinary measures based on the student's offense history.\par
\par
\b Figure 27. All Cases List\b0\par
Figure 27 shows the All Cases List module of the School Violation System. This module displays all recorded student violation cases stored in the system in one centralized location. Through this feature, the administrator can view essential information such as the student's name, violation committed, date of incident, and case status. The system organizes all records into a manageable list, enabling faster review and easier monitoring of disciplinary cases. Once a case is recorded, it automatically becomes part of this list, where the administrator can access it for further action, verification, or follow-up.\par
\par
\b Figure 28. Case Details\b0\par
Figure 28 shows the Case Details module of the School Violation System. This module is used to present the complete information of a selected disciplinary case. Through this feature, the administrator can examine the student's information, the specific violation committed, the description of the incident, the offense history, the assigned sanction, attached evidence, and other related case records. The system retrieves all case-related data from the database and displays them in a single view, allowing the administrator to review the incident thoroughly. This helps ensure that every disciplinary decision is based on complete and properly documented information.\par
\par
\b Figure 29. Violation Catalog\b0\par
Figure 29 shows the Violation Catalog module of the School Violation System. This module contains the official and standardized list of violations recognized by the institution. Through this feature, the administrator can view or manage violation records that include the violation code, title, category, severity, and corresponding sanctions for different offense levels. The system uses this catalog during case recording to ensure that violations are selected from a predefined and standardized set of offenses. This helps maintain consistency in documentation and accuracy in assigning sanctions across all recorded cases.\par
\par
\b Figure 30. Student Import\b0\par
Figure 30 shows the Student Import module of the School Violation System. This module is used to upload multiple student records into the system efficiently instead of encoding them manually one by one. Through this feature, the administrator can import a prepared file containing student information, which the system then processes and stores in the database. Once the import is completed, the student records become available in other modules such as student search, violation recording, and record retrieval. This feature supports faster data setup and reduces the time and effort required in maintaining student records.\par
\par
\b Figure 31. Record Retrieval\b0\par
Figure 31 shows the Record Retrieval module of the School Violation System. This module is used to search and retrieve stored disciplinary records quickly and efficiently. Through this feature, the administrator can locate a specific violation record by entering search terms or applying filters such as student name, department, violation type, case status, academic year, or date range. The system then scans the database and displays the matching results. This makes the retrieval of disciplinary records more convenient, organized, and accessible for review, validation, and reference.\par
\par
\b Objective 2: Improving Case Management and Monitoring\b0\par
\par
\b Figure 32. Dashboard Overview\b0\par
Figure 32 shows the Dashboard Overview module of the School Violation System. This module provides the administrator with a summarized view of the overall disciplinary condition of the institution. Through this feature, the system displays important data such as total recorded cases, open cases, closed cases, hearing schedules, and recent case activities. The system automatically gathers information from the stored records and presents it through summary cards and visual components, allowing the administrator to monitor the current status of student violations at a glance. This module improves efficiency by giving users immediate access to key disciplinary information.\par
\par
\b Figure 33. Record Violation Step 2: Select Violation\b0\par
Figure 33 shows the Select Violation step in the violation recording process of the School Violation System. This step is used to identify the specific offense committed by the student after the student record has been selected. Through this feature, the administrator chooses the appropriate violation from the list of registered offenses available in the system. The displayed list includes the violation code, title, and severity level, helping the user select the most accurate offense for the incident being recorded. This step ensures that the correct disciplinary violation is linked to the student case before the administrator proceeds to the incident details and sanction preview.\par
\par
\b Figure 34. Edit Case\b0\par
Figure 34 shows the Edit Case module of the School Violation System. This module is used to update or correct an existing student violation record whenever necessary. Through this feature, the administrator can review previously entered case details and make changes to the information if errors are found or if additional updates are required. Once the updated details are submitted, the system saves the revised information in the database and reflects the changes in the case record. This module helps maintain the accuracy, completeness, and reliability of disciplinary records throughout the monitoring process.\par
\par
\b Figure 35. Student Profile with Violations\b0\par
Figure 35 shows the Student Profile with Violations module of the School Violation System. This module presents a student's personal information together with his or her disciplinary history in one consolidated view. Through this feature, the administrator can review the student's profile, previous violation records, case statuses, and related sanctions. The system retrieves all cases linked to the selected student and displays them in an organized format, allowing the administrator to examine patterns of behavior and identify repeat offenses. This feature supports more informed disciplinary decisions and better monitoring of student conduct over time.\par
\par
\b Figure 36. Cases Search and Filter\b0\par
Figure 36 shows the Cases Search and Filter feature of the School Violation System. This feature is used to help the administrator narrow down and organize stored case records based on specific conditions. Through this feature, the administrator can enter keywords and apply filters such as status, severity, or other available criteria to refine the case list. Once the search term or filter is applied, the system updates the displayed results and shows only the matching cases. This makes it easier to locate specific records without manually browsing the full case list, thereby improving the speed and efficiency of case management.\par
\par
\b Figure 37. Students Search\b0\par
Figure 37 shows the Students Search module of the School Violation System. This module is used to quickly locate student records stored in the database. Through this feature, the administrator can search for a student by name, student number, email, or other identifying information. After the search is performed, the system displays the matching student records, allowing the administrator to select the correct profile for review, violation recording, or case monitoring. This feature helps streamline access to student information and supports faster retrieval of records during disciplinary processing.\par
\par
\b Figure 38. Advanced Record Retrieval\b0\par
Figure 38 shows the Advanced Record Retrieval module of the School Violation System. This module extends the search and retrieval process by allowing the administrator to use more specific and detailed filtering options. Through this feature, disciplinary records can be retrieved based on criteria such as department, violation type, academic year, case status, and date range. Once the selected filters are applied, the system processes the data and presents the exact matching records. This supports more accurate review, monitoring, and documentation of disciplinary cases, especially when dealing with a large number of stored records.\par
\par
\b Objective 3: Reports, Analytics, and Decision-Making\b0\par
\par
\b Figure 39. System Reports and Trends\b0\par
Figure 39 shows the System Reports and Trends module of the School Violation System. This module provides summarized reports and trend-based analysis derived from the recorded disciplinary cases in the system. Through this feature, the administrator can view totals, comparisons, and case patterns over a period of time. The system automatically processes the stored data and presents it in a structured format, making it easier to determine whether student violations are increasing, decreasing, or remaining stable. This module supports administrative evaluation by transforming raw case records into meaningful information.\par
\par
\b Figure 40. Department Top Violations\b0\par
Figure 40 shows the Department Top Violations report of the School Violation System. This module is used to identify the departments with the highest number of recorded cases and the most common violations committed within those departments. Through this feature, the system groups stored disciplinary records according to department and violation type, then calculates the most frequent offenses. The results are displayed in a summarized format that helps school officials analyze where disciplinary concerns are more concentrated. This supports better planning of interventions and more focused disciplinary monitoring across academic departments.\par
\par
\b Figure 41. Sanctions Compliance\b0\par
Figure 41 shows the Sanctions Compliance module of the School Violation System. This module is used to monitor whether the sanctions assigned to student disciplinary cases have already been completed or are still pending. Through this feature, the system reviews recorded cases and compares their status with the sanctions imposed. It then provides a summary of completed, pending, and unresolved disciplinary actions. This enables administrators to ensure that sanctions are properly implemented and followed through, which strengthens accountability and improves the overall disciplinary process.\par
\par
\b Figure 42. Violation Ledger and Exports\b0\par
Figure 42 shows the Violation Ledger and Exports module of the School Violation System. This module provides a formal listing of recorded student violation cases and supports the generation of exportable reports. Through this feature, the administrator can view filtered disciplinary records and produce output in formats such as PDF, CSV, or print view for documentation and official reporting purposes. Once the records are generated, they can be used for filing, presentation, or submission to school authorities. This feature improves the documentation process by making disciplinary data easier to prepare, organize, and distribute.\par
\par
\b Figure 43. Dashboard Violation Trends\b0\par
Figure 43 shows the Dashboard Violation Trends feature of the School Violation System. This feature is used to present visual summaries of student violation patterns directly on the dashboard. Through this feature, the system processes stored case data and converts them into charts or trend indicators that show the volume and direction of disciplinary incidents over time. This allows administrators to quickly identify significant changes in violation activity and use the information for planning interventions or strengthening disciplinary policies. It enhances data interpretation by presenting complex information in a more understandable visual form.\par
\par
\b Figure 44. Printable Violation Report\b0\par
Figure 44 shows the Printable Violation Report module of the School Violation System. This module is used to prepare selected disciplinary records in a print-ready and formal report format. Through this feature, the administrator can generate a structured report containing the necessary details of recorded violation cases for official school documentation. Once the report is produced, it can be reviewed, printed, or saved for institutional use. This module supports proper reporting and preserves disciplinary records in a format suitable for presentation, filing, and administrative reference.\par
\par
\b Figure 45. Student Violation Summary\b0\par
Figure 45 shows the Student Violation Summary module of the School Violation System. This module provides a consolidated report of all disciplinary cases associated with a specific student. Through this feature, the system retrieves the student's complete violation history, including the offenses committed, dates recorded, current statuses, and corresponding sanctions. The information is then presented in an organized summary format that can be used for case evaluation, counseling reference, hearing preparation, or future disciplinary action. This module helps administrators and school officials gain a clearer understanding of a student's behavioral record and supports more informed decision-making.\par
\par
\b Summary\b0\par
Figures 25 to 45 present the major modules of the School Violation System based on the three objectives of the study. Collectively, these modules demonstrate how the system supports the accurate recording of student violations, the organized management and monitoring of disciplinary cases, and the generation of meaningful reports for evaluation and decision-making. Through these integrated features, the system contributes to a more systematic, efficient, and reliable approach to student disciplinary management within the institution.\par
